= a
{parent=1}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

For a separated <harmonic function> $R(r)\Theta(\theta)$ in <polar coordinates>, single-valuedness forces integer angular frequencies. The radial factors for frequency $m\geq1$ are $r^m,r^{-m}$, while frequency zero has a constant and $\ln r$. Finiteness at the origin excludes $r^{-m}$ and $\ln r$.

The source's angular factor is $\cos\theta$ in every term, as printed, rather than $\cos(n\theta)$. The <power-law angular mode for the planar Poisson equation> gives
$$
\Delta[r^{n+2}\cos\theta]=[(n+2)^2-1]r^n\cos\theta=(n+1)(n+3)r^n\cos\theta.
$$
Hence, assuming the series permits termwise differentiation, the general finite, single-valued solution is
$$
\boxed{\phi=C+\sum_{m=1}^\infty r^m[A_m\cos(m\theta)+B_m\sin(m\theta)]+\cos\theta\sum_{n=0}^\infty\frac{\alpha_n r^{n+2}}{(n+1)(n+3)}.}
$$
The arbitrary harmonic series must itself converge to a finite function on the prescribed disk. If $\alpha_0\ne0$, the source has a direction-dependent limit at the origin; the displayed potential still extends finitely there, but the equation at that point is understood weakly rather than as a smooth classical source.

For the special source, a particular potential is $r^3\cos\theta/8$. Its boundary cosine term is canceled by $-a^2r\cos\theta/8$, and the boundary sine term is supplied by $(r/a)^2\sin2\theta$. The resulting <Dirichlet boundary conditions> has solution
$$
\boxed{\phi(r,\theta)=\frac{r(r^2-a^2)}8\cos\theta+\frac{r^2}{a^2}\sin2\theta.}
$$
It is finite at zero, has the required boundary values, and its <Laplacian> is $r\cos\theta$.

= b
{parent=1}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

The far radial derivative selects the growing frequency-one term $Ur\cos\theta$. The matching decaying harmonic is $B\cos\theta/r$. The no-penetration condition gives $\partial_r\phi=(U-B/r^2)\cos\theta=0$ at $r=a$, so $B=Ua^2$. Thus the <potential flow around a circular cylinder> is
$$
\boxed{\phi(r,\theta)=U\left(r+\frac{a^2}{r}\right)\cos\theta+C.}
$$
The remaining single-valued exterior harmonics are excluded by the zero radial derivative at the cylinder and the prescribed derivative at infinity, apart from the arbitrary additive constant. In particular the logarithmic radial term would have a nonzero radial derivative on the cylinder. A circulation term proportional to $\theta$ is excluded by single-valuedness of the potential.

= 2
{parent=Paper 2}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=2}

Use <Green's second identity> in the source coordinate:
$$
\int_\Omega(u\Delta v-v\Delta u)dV'=\int_{\partial\Omega}(u\partial_n v-v\partial_n u)dS'.
$$
Take $u(\mathbf x')=\psi(\mathbf x')$ and $v(\mathbf x')=-1/[4\pi|\mathbf x-\mathbf x'|]$, the <fundamental solution of the Laplace equation> satisfying $\Delta'v=\delta(\mathbf x'-\mathbf x)$. With localized charge and the decaying potential, the boundary integral tends to zero on expanding spheres. The volume identity then gives $\psi(\mathbf x)+(1/\epsilon_0)\int vq\,dV'=0$, hence the <Coulomb potential>
$$
\boxed{\psi(\mathbf x)=\frac1{4\pi\epsilon_0}\int_{\mathbb R^3}\frac{q(\mathbf x')}{|\mathbf x-\mathbf x'|}dV'.}
$$
Convergence of this integral and a condition fixing the potential at infinity are needed: mere pointwise decay of $q$ is not enough, and a harmonic function could otherwise be added. A bounded, compactly supported charge distribution is a sufficient setting for this derivation.

For the disk put $\sigma=Q/(\pi a^2)$. On its axis, every ring of radius $\rho$ is at distance $\sqrt{\rho^2+z^2}$, so
$$
\boxed{\psi(0,z)=\frac{\sigma}{2\epsilon_0}\int_0^a\frac{\rho\,d\rho}{\sqrt{\rho^2+z^2}}=\frac{Q}{2\pi\epsilon_0a^2}(\sqrt{a^2+z^2}-|z|).}
$$
For $z>0$ subtract $z$; for $z<0$ add $z$. This is the <electrostatic potential of a uniformly charged disk> with zero potential at infinity. The absolute value is essential for reflection symmetry and the correct behavior on the lower axis.

\Image[paper-2-charged-disk-axis.png]
{title=The potential is continuous across a charged disk while its normal field jumps}
{description=Original axis potential and normal electric field for a uniform positive surface charge. Open circles at the center show the two limiting fields, not a single field value assigned on the ideal sheet.}

= i
{parent=2}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

Write $A=Q/(2\pi\epsilon_0a^2)$. The <Taylor expansion> $\sqrt{a^2+z^2}=a+z^2/(2a)+O(z^4/a^3)$ gives the near-axis result
$$
\boxed{\psi(0,z)=A\left[a-|z|+\frac{z^2}{2a}+O(z^4/a^3)\right].}
$$
To extend off the axis, work separately above and below the charged plane. In either source-free half-space the regular axisymmetric <solid harmonics> are $r^nP_n(\cos\theta)$. Matching the positive-axis coefficients, using $P_n(1)=1$, and then reflecting in the disk gives
$$
\boxed{\psi(r,\theta)=A\left[a-r|\cos\theta|+\frac{r^2}{2a}P_2(\cos\theta)+O(r^4/a^3)\right],\qquad r\ll a.}
$$
Here $P_2(t)=(3t^2-1)/2$ is a <Legendre polynomial>. The quadratic term is $A(z^2-\rho^2/2)/(2a)$. The linear term is $-Az$ above and $+Az$ below; it represents the discontinuity of the normal <electric field>. A single regular spherical expansion across the entire charged sheet would incorrectly remove that jump.

= ii
{parent=2}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

For large $|z|$, expand in $a^2/z^2$ to obtain
$$
\boxed{\psi(0,z)=\frac{Q}{4\pi\epsilon_0}\left[\frac1{|z|}-\frac{a^2}{4|z|^3}+O(a^4/|z|^5)\right].}
$$
The axisymmetric exterior <multipole expansion> replaces each $|z|^{-n-1}$ on the positive axis by $r^{-n-1}P_n(\cos\theta)$. Reflection symmetry eliminates the odd multipoles, so
$$
\boxed{\psi(r,\theta)=\frac{Q}{4\pi\epsilon_0}\left[\frac1r-\frac{a^2}{4r^3}P_2(\cos\theta)+O(a^4/r^5)\right],\qquad r\gg a.}
$$
The leading term is the potential of total charge $Q$ concentrated at the center; the next is its <electric quadrupole> correction. On either axis $P_2(\pm1)=1$, recovering the same even axis potential.

= 3
{parent=Paper 2}
{scope}

= a
{parent=3}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

For a complex derivative to exist, the difference quotient must agree along real and imaginary increments. These give $u_x+iv_x$ and $v_y-iu_y$, respectively. Equating their real and imaginary parts gives the <Cauchy-Riemann equations>
$$
\boxed{u_x=v_y,\qquad u_y=-v_x.}
$$
For $g(z)=z\operatorname{Re}z$, the components are $u=x^2$ and $v=xy$. The first equation requires $2x=x$, so $x=0$; the second requires $0=-y$, so $y=0$. Thus zero is the only possible differentiability point. Directly,
$$
\frac{g(h)-g(0)}h=\operatorname{Re}h\longrightarrow0,
$$
so $\boxed{g'(0)=0}$. The function is differentiable at that isolated point but is not <holomorphic> on any neighborhood of it.

= b
{parent=3}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

Use the <chain rule> with $x=r\cos\theta$, $y=r\sin\theta$. Then $U_r=u_x\cos\theta+u_y\sin\theta$ and $V_\theta=-rv_x\sin\theta+rv_y\cos\theta$. Substituting the Cartesian <Cauchy-Riemann equations> gives $V_\theta=rU_r$. Similarly $V_r=v_x\cos\theta+v_y\sin\theta=-U_\theta/r$. Thus, for $r>0$, the polar equations are
$$
\boxed{U_r=\frac1rV_\theta,\qquad V_r=-\frac1rU_\theta.}
$$
The restriction $r>0$ is needed because the polar chart degenerates at the origin.

