= i
{parent=c}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

On the unit circle use $z=e^{i\theta}$ with $0<\theta<2\pi$. The specified branch has $\operatorname{Log}z=i\theta$ there and $dz=ie^{i\theta}d\theta$. Therefore
$$
\boxed{I=-\int_0^{2\pi}\theta e^{i\theta}d\theta=-[(1-i\theta)e^{i\theta}]_0^{2\pi}=2\pi i.}
$$
This is the <circle integral of a logarithm with a radial branch cut>, using the one-sided values at the cut crossing. The logarithm is discontinuous at that crossing, so the <Cauchy integral theorem> for a function holomorphic around a closed contour does not apply.

\Image[paper-2-logarithm-branch-circles.png]
{title=The cut-crossing point determines the circle integral of a logarithm}
{description=Original unit-circle illustrations of the two radial branch cuts. The formula is $2\pi i$ times the cut-crossing point; the second circle represents the general radius-$R$ case by rescaling.}

= ii
{parent=c}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

Choose the argument interval $-3\pi/2<\theta<\pi/2$, which makes the logarithm real on the positive real axis and puts its cut on the positive imaginary axis. Parametrize $z=Re^{i\theta}$. The constant term $\ln R$ contributes $\ln R\oint dz=0$, leaving
$$
I=-R\int_{-3\pi/2}^{\pi/2}\theta e^{i\theta}d\theta=\boxed{-2\pi R.}
$$
Equivalently the cut-crossing point is $iR$, so the <circle integral of a logarithm with a radial branch cut> is $2\pi i(iR)=-2\pi R$. The different cut changes where the discontinuity lies on the circle; it is not merely an additive constant change of branch along the entire contour.

= 4
{parent=Paper 2}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=4}

The <residue theorem> states that a positively oriented simple closed contour enclosing isolated poles and none on the contour has integral $2\pi i$ times the sum of their <residues>, provided the function is holomorphic elsewhere inside. Near a simple zero of $\psi$, $\psi(z)=\psi'(a)(z-a)+O((z-a)^2)$. Hence
$$
\boxed{\operatorname{Res}_{z=a}\frac{\phi(z)}{\psi(z)}=\lim_{z\to a}\frac{(z-a)\phi(z)}{\psi(z)}=\frac{\phi(a)}{\psi'(a)}.}
$$
For $\cot z=\cos z/\sin z$, this gives $\boxed{\operatorname{Res}_{z=k\pi}\cot z=1}$ for every integer $k$.

To evaluate the real integral, integrate $F(z)=\operatorname{Log}z/(z^2+a^2)$ around an upper semicircle, with a small upper semicircular indentation excluding zero. Use the branch real on the positive axis and with argument between zero and $\pi$ in the upper half-plane. The large arc tends to zero as $O(\ln R/R)$ and the small arc as $O(\epsilon|\ln\epsilon|)$. The only enclosed pole is $ia$, whose contribution is
$$
2\pi i\operatorname{Res}_{z=ia}F=\frac\pi a\left(\ln a+\frac{i\pi}{2}\right).
$$
On the negative real axis the upper boundary value is $\operatorname{Log}(-x)=\ln x+i\pi$. The two real segments therefore contribute
$$
2I+i\pi\int_0^\infty\frac{dx}{x^2+a^2}=2I+\frac{i\pi^2}{2a}.
$$
Comparing real parts yields
$$
\boxed{I=\int_0^\infty\frac{\ln x}{x^2+a^2}dx=\frac{\pi\ln a}{2a},\qquad I|_{a=1}=0.}
$$
The integral is absolutely convergent at both zero and infinity, so no principal-value interpretation is needed.

= 5
{parent=Paper 2}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=5}

For causal functions the <convolution> is $(f*g)(t)=\int_0^t f(\tau)g(t-\tau)d\tau$. In a half-plane of absolute convergence, changing variables $u=t-\tau$ in its <Laplace transform> and using <Fubini's theorem> gives
$$
\mathcal L(f*g)(s)=\int_0^\infty\int_0^\infty e^{-s(u+\tau)}f(\tau)g(u)du\,d\tau=F(s)G(s).
$$
This proves the <convolution theorem for Laplace transforms>. Transforming the differential equation gives $(s^2+1)Y(s)=sy(0)+y'(0)+F(s)$. The direct transform pairs $\mathcal L(\cos t)=s/(s^2+1)$ and $\mathcal L(\sin t)=1/(s^2+1)$ then give
$$
\boxed{y(t)=y(0)\cos t+y'(0)\sin t+\int_0^t f(\tau)\sin(t-\tau)d\tau.}
$$
For a continuous forcing, call the last term $w(t)$. The <Leibniz integral rule> gives $w'=\int_0^t f(\tau)\cos(t-\tau)d\tau$ and $w''=f(t)-w(t)$. Also $w(0)=w'(0)=0$. Thus both the original equation and the initial values hold.

For an impulse at positive time $T$, the <Dirac delta> evaluates the convolution, yielding
$$
\boxed{y(t)=y(0)\cos t+y'(0)\sin t+H(t-T)\sin(t-T).}
$$
Here $H$ is the <Heaviside step function>. The displacement remains continuous at $T$, while its derivative jumps upward by one, exactly as required by integrating the differential equation across the unit impulse.

= 6
{parent=Paper 2}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=6}

An <isotropic tensor> has unchanged components under every rotation of the Cartesian axes. Under proper rotations in three dimensions, rank one has **no nonzero isotropic tensor**, rank two has $\boxed{T_{ij}=A\delta_{ij}}$, and rank three has $\boxed{T_{ijk}=B\epsilon_{ijk}}$. Here $\delta$ is the <Kronecker delta> and $\epsilon$ the <Levi-Civita symbol>. If invariance under reflections is also required for a true tensor, the rank-three case vanishes; $\epsilon$ is then a pseudotensor.

The <velocity gradient tensor> for the given field is
$$
D_{ik}=\frac{\partial u_i}{\partial x_k}=\begin{pmatrix}x_2^2&2x_1x_2&0\\0&x_3^2&2x_2x_3\\2x_1x_3&0&x_1^2\end{pmatrix}.
$$
Its symmetric part, the <strain-rate tensor> if $\mathbf u$ is interpreted as a velocity, is
$$
\boxed{e=\frac{D+D^T}{2}=\begin{pmatrix}x_2^2&x_1x_2&x_1x_3\\x_1x_2&x_3^2&x_2x_3\\x_1x_3&x_2x_3&x_1^2\end{pmatrix}.}
$$
The antisymmetric part has the specified ordering $D_{ik}-e_{ik}=\epsilon_{ijk}\omega_j$, with
$$
\boxed{\boldsymbol\omega=\tfrac12\nabla\times\mathbf u=(-x_2x_3,-x_1x_3,-x_1x_2).}
$$
The index ordering matters for this sign; it agrees with a local rotation velocity $\boldsymbol\omega\times\mathbf x$. This is the axial part of the <scalar, symmetric-traceless and axial tensor decomposition>, retaining the trace inside the symmetric part here.

At the specified point, $e=\begin{pmatrix}9&6&0\\6&0&0\\0&0&4\end{pmatrix}$. The upper block has characteristic polynomial $\lambda^2-9\lambda-36=(\lambda-12)(\lambda+3)$. Thus the principal values and unit <eigenvectors> are
$$
\boxed{12:\ \frac{(2,1,0)}{\sqrt5};\qquad -3:\ \frac{(1,-2,0)}{\sqrt5};\qquad 4:\ (0,0,1).}
$$
These mutually orthogonal directions are the <principal strain rates>' axes for this local field; reversing an eigenvector does not change its axis.

= 7
{parent=Paper 2}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=7}

A <normal mode> has all coordinates following one fixed displacement pattern with a common time factor. Its <normal frequency> is that factor's oscillation frequency; <normal mode coordinates> are amplitudes in a basis of such patterns, making the linear equations independent. A zero-frequency mode can instead have constant velocity along a symmetry direction.

For the unit circle, each angular displacement equals its corresponding arc displacement. The <kinetic energy> and change in <potential energy> are
$$
T=\frac m2(\dot\theta_1^2+\dot\theta_2^2+\mu\dot\theta_3^2),\qquad V-V_0=\frac k2[(\theta_2-\theta_1)^2+(\theta_3-\theta_2)^2+(\theta_1-\theta_3)^2].
$$
Any common spring prestress changes only the constant $V_0$, because the sum of the three arc lengths is fixed. The <Euler-Lagrange equations> give
$$
m\ddot\theta_1+k(2\theta_1-\theta_2-\theta_3)=0,\qquad m\ddot\theta_2+k(2\theta_2-\theta_1-\theta_3)=0,\qquad\mu m\ddot\theta_3+k(2\theta_3-\theta_1-\theta_2)=0.
$$
These are the <normal modes of three beads connected by springs on a circular hoop>, with third mass $M=\mu m$. For $\mu>0$, their three mode vectors and squared <angular frequencies> are
$$
\boxed{v_0=(1,1,1),\ \omega_0^2=0;\qquad v_A=(1,-1,0),\ \omega_A^2=\frac{3k}{m};\qquad v_S=(1,1,-2/\mu),\ \omega_S^2=\frac{k}{m}\left(1+\frac2\mu\right).}
$$
The zero mode rotates the entire configuration without stretching a spring. The antisymmetric mode moves the equal masses oppositely while leaving the third fixed. In the symmetric mode the equal masses move together against the third, with zero total <angular momentum>. These vectors are orthogonal in the mass inner product with matrix $m\operatorname{diag}(1,1,\mu)$.

Writing $\boldsymbol\theta=Q_0v_0+Q_Av_A+Q_Sv_S$ gives independent equations $\ddot Q_0=0$, $\ddot Q_A+\omega_A^2Q_A=0$, $\ddot Q_S+\omega_S^2Q_S=0$. In particular $Q_0=A+Bt$, retaining possible slow common rotation. For $\mu=1$, the two positive frequencies coincide at $\sqrt{3k/m}$; their two-dimensional space is all patterns with $\theta_1+\theta_2+\theta_3=0$.

\Image[paper-2-three-bead-modes.png]
{title=Rigid rotation and the two oscillatory patterns of three spring-coupled beads}
{description=Original illustrations for the example $\mu=2$. Arrows are tangent displacements at the equilibrium positions; spring extensions depend on differences of neighboring angular displacements.}

= 8
{parent=Paper 2}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=8}

A <right coset> of $H$ in $G$ is $Hg=\{hg:h\in H\}$. Right multiplication by $g$ is a <bijection> $H\to Hg$, with inverse $x\mapsto xg^{-1}$, so every right coset has $|H|$ elements. The analogous map $h\mapsto gh$ proves the same assertion for <left cosets>.

Write the displayed matrices as $M(a,b,c)$. Direct multiplication gives
$$
M(a,b,c)M(A,B,C)=M(a+A,b+B+aC,c+C).
$$
All new coordinates are integers, so the set is closed. Matrix multiplication is associative, the identity is $M(0,0,0)$, and
$$
\boxed{M(a,b,c)^{-1}=M(-a,ac-b,-c),}
$$
which also has integer coordinates. Thus it is the <Integer Heisenberg group>.

The subset $H=\{M(0,0,t):t\in\mathbb Z\}$ contains the identity, and multiplication and inversion add or negate $t$, so it is a <subgroup>. Its <right cosets> are
$$
\boxed{HM(a,b,c)=\{M(a,b,c+t):t\in\mathbb Z\}=\{M(a,b,s):s\in\mathbb Z\}.}
$$
They are exactly the sets with fixed integer coordinates $(a,b)$, as described by <cosets of a coordinate subgroup of the integer Heisenberg group>. The factor order $HM$ is essential; multiplying by $H$ on the other side would also change the upper-right coordinate by $at$.

= 9
{parent=Paper 2}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=9}

Let $r$ be a quarter-turn and $s$ a reflection of the square. The <dihedral group> is generated by $r,s$ with $r^4=s^2=1$ and $srs=r^{-1}$, and its elements are $1,r,r^2,r^3,s,sr,sr^2,sr^3$. Two generators are minimal because this group is not cyclic. Its subgroups of order two are $\langle r^2\rangle$ and $\langle sr^j\rangle$ for $j=0,1,2,3$. Its subgroups of order four are $\langle r\rangle$, $\langle r^2,s\rangle$ and $\langle r^2,sr\rangle$.

A <group homomorphism> obeys $\phi(gh)=\phi(g)\phi(h)$ for all elements; a <group isomorphism> is a bijective group homomorphism. Take the rotation subgroup $\langle r\rangle$ and define
$$
\boxed{\phi(r^j)=i^j\in Q,\qquad \ker\phi=\{1\}.}
$$
The quaternion element $i$ has order four, so the map is well defined modulo four, preserves products and is injective onto $\{1,i,-1,-i\}$. Surjectivity onto all eight quaternion elements is not required of a homomorphism.

A <cyclic group> is generated by the powers of one element. The final claim needs the usual qualification **no nontrivial proper subgroup**, and $|G|>1$: the <trivial subgroup> is proper in every nontrivial group, even one of prime order. With that interpretation, if $G=\langle g\rangle$ has prime order $p$, <Lagrange's theorem> permits only subgroup orders one and $p$. Conversely, if its order $n>1$ is composite, choose a divisor $d$ with $1<d<n$. The subgroup $\langle g^d\rangle$ has order $n/d$, strictly between one and $n$. This proves the assertion described by <groups with no proper nontrivial subgroups>.

= 10
{parent=Paper 2}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=10}

A <group representation> is a homomorphism $D:G\to\operatorname{GL}(V)$ into invertible linear maps. It is a <faithful representation> when it is injective, equivalently when its kernel contains only the identity. A faithful real two-dimensional representation of the <cyclic group of order four> is
$$
\boxed{D(1)=I,\quad D(i)=J=\begin{pmatrix}0&-1\\1&0\end{pmatrix},\quad D(-1)=-I,\quad D(-i)=-J.}
$$
These are rotations of the plane through $0,\pi/2,\pi,3\pi/2$. Since $J^2=-I$ and $J^4=I$, their multiplication agrees with the group, and all four matrices are distinct.

For any invertible $S$, set $\widetilde D(g)=SD(g)S^{-1}$. Then
$$
\widetilde D(g)\widetilde D(h)=SD(g)S^{-1}SD(h)S^{-1}=SD(gh)S^{-1}=\widetilde D(gh),
$$
so this also is a <group representation>. The <character of a representation> is $\chi_D(g)=\operatorname{tr}D(g)$. For the displayed quarter-turn representation, its values on $1,i,-1,-i$ are $2,0,-2,0$.

= i
{parent=10}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

The <trace> is invariant under a <similarity transformation>, by cyclicity of the trace:
$$
\boxed{\chi_{\widetilde D}(g)=\operatorname{tr}(SD(g)S^{-1})=\operatorname{tr}(D(g)S^{-1}S)=\chi_D(g).}
$$
Hence <equivalent representations> have the same <character of a representation>.

= ii
{parent=10}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

If $h=aga^{-1}$, the representation law gives $D(h)=D(a)D(g)D(a)^{-1}$. Trace invariance under this <similarity transformation> gives
$$
\boxed{\chi_D(h)=\chi_D(g).}
$$
Thus the <character of a representation> is constant on every <conjugacy class>.
